    assert (this->successful);
    unsigned int len = (this->head - this->start)
                     + (this->end  - this->tail);
    char *p = (char *) malloc (len);
```

The output copies head area (`start` to `head`) and tail area (`tail` to `end`). The head area is where the "root" object is serialized (forward-growing). The tail area is where packed objects are (backward-growing).

When `pop_pack()` moves data from head to tail:
```cpp
obj->head = tail - len;
memmove (obj->head, head, len);
tail -= len;
```

It copies the data that was serialized in the head area to the tail area. Since `allocate_size` zeros and then the code writes to it, the bytes in the head area should be initialized.

Hmm, but there's another possibility. What about the `name` table's trick:

```cpp
auto snap = c->snapshot ();
this->nameRecordZ.serialize (c, this->count);
this->stringOffset = c->length ();
c->revert (snap);
```

After `revert`, those bytes that were serialized are still in the buffer but head is moved back. If the next operations don't reach those same bytes, they could remain from the previous write. But `allocate_size` always zeroes, so even after revert, when new data is written to the same region, it gets zeroed again.

